B2 build is a perfect build system to create, manage, upgrade c++ project.

Read first: [link build-install-b2-build Build and Install B2 Build] .

[h1:executable-project [role cyan Create, manage c++ executable project]]

[*Start: create project directory]

```
mkdir my-prj
cd my-prj
```

[*Write Jamfile]

[role blue [*File: jamroot]]

```
exe hello : hello.cpp : <define>cpp_hot : <cxxstd>26 ;
```

[*exe] is a rule to define an executable target: [*hello]

[role ddgreen [*In b2 jamfiles, many rules have five parameters, splitted by a colon keyword]] [role red [*:]] , [*(A colon is a keyword in b2 jamfiles.)]

Note that both the left side and right side of : and ; must be blank characters.

* [role red First parameter: [*hello]][br]
First parameter is commonly the [role dred [*target name]] .
* [role red Second parameter: [*hello.cpp]][br]
Second parameter is commonly the [role dred [*source list]]: c++ source file list; and also, another target can be put to the source list too.
* [role red Third parameter: [*<define>cpp_hot]][br]
Third parameter commonly lists some [role dred [*requirements]] that are required to build current target, these requirements can not be overrided on commandline. [*These requirements can be named as [role dred requirements]] .
* [role red Fourth parameter: [*<cxxstd>26]][br]
4th parameter commonly lists some preferences that are used to build current target, these preferences can be overrided on commandline. There preferences can be named as [role dred [*default-build]]
* [role red 5th parameter: none in this example][br]
5th parameter lists some requirements required by another target that depends on current target: If another target depends on current target, these requirements will be applied to another target. It is named [role dred [*usage-requirements]] .

[*Write c++]

[role blue File: hello.cpp]

[!c++]
```
#include <iostream>

int main()
{
	std::cout << "Hello, c++!" << std::endl;
}
```

[*Build project]

Just run command b2:

```
> b2
```

[*Run program:]

The compiled program is generated at folder bin, you have to find it.

```
> ./bin/gcc-16/debug/cxxstd-26-iso/hello
Hello, c++!
```

[h1:library-project [role cyan Create, manage c++ library project]]

[role ddred [*Init project folders:]]

```
mkdir myprj
cd myprj
mkdir include src
```

[role ddred [*File: jamroot]]

```
lib foo : src/foo.cpp : <cxxstd>26 <include>./include : : <include>./include ;

exe prog : prog.cpp : <library>foo ;
```

rule [*lib] defines a target [*foo].

Because [*foo] is a [*lib] target, the b2 build will generate [*libfoo.so] by default on linux, or related shared library on windows.

[*<cxxstd>26] in the [*requirements] list: it can not be overrided on commandline (in the 3rd paramter);

[*<include>./include] in the [*requirements] list of [*third parameter]: building current library requires using ./inlcude as include path,[br]
which is translated to [role ddblue [*-I./include]][br]
;

The 4th parameter is empty;

[*<include>./include] in the list of [*5th parameter]: if another target depends on this target, [*<include>./include] will be applied to another target;

[role ddred [*File: include/foo.hpp]]

[!c++]
```
#pragma once

namespace dir
{
	class foo
	{
	public:
		void print() const;
	};
};
```

[role ddred [*File: src/foo.cpp]]

[!c++]
```
#include <foo.hpp>
#include <iostream>

void dir::foo::print() const
{
	std::cout << "Hello, c++ world!" << std::endl;
}
```

[role ddred [*File: prog.cpp]]

[!c++]
```
#include <foo.hpp>

int main()
{
	dir::foo object;
	object.print();
}
```

[role ddred [*Build project:]]

Just run command b2

```
b2
```

[role ddred [*Run program:]]

The program prog is generated in bin folder, you have to find where it is.

```
> ./bin/gcc-16/debug/prog
Hello, c++ world!
>
```

